WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.688 --> 00:00:03.271
(upbeat music)

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.150
- What's up, guys? Michael here.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:08.680
The saying, "geek culture is pop culture"

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:11.480
has become a cliche, and with good reason.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.620
Superheroes rule the box office,

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.710
video games bring in
more money than movies,

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:18.330
and all the cool kids play tabletop RPGs.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.480
It seems the mainstream has
shaken off its affected smirk

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.660
and dived full-force
into unabashed nerd-dom.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.290
What's more, the ways we interact

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:28.190
with pop culture have also changed,

00:00:28.190 --> 00:00:29.990
reflecting the behaviors of geek culture

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:31.700
that normies once shunned.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.310
We used to make fun of Trekkies,

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:35.000
and comic book fans were roasted

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.580
for obsessing over the
finer details of continuity.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:40.830
Now, you can't even watch a
movie where cars go vroom!

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.720
without taking on a laundry list

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.430
of prior movies and spin-offs.

00:00:44.430 --> 00:00:46.190
Wanting to play video games for a living

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:49.080
was once equivalent to
wanting to be a Power Ranger.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.290
Now, E-sports stars make a good chunk

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:52.930
of what professional athletes make,

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:54.300
without the concussions.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.340
And going to bat for
your favorite franchise

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.060
is now standard practice.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:00.110
- [Male] I wanna talk
about "Star Craft II",

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:02.702
in many ways the last hurrah
of the single-player RTS

00:01:02.702 --> 00:01:06.340
and, if you ask me, the
rightful queen of the genre.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.960
- So how did geek culture
become ubiquitous?

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.490
And does geek culture actually still exist

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:12.950
once it's so mainstream?

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:15.240
Get yourself comfy for
this Wisecrack Edition

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.810
Is Geek Culture Dead?

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:19.600
Since the beginning, geek
subculture was concerned with

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.430
and unified primarily by media,
especially genre fiction,

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:26.430
which means that geek culture
has been inextricably tied

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:27.770
to the concept of fandom.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.610
Important note, geek culture
overlaps with fan culture,

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.210
but doesn't encompass all fandom.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:35.930
Basically, all geeks are fans,
but not all fans are geeks.

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:39.290
Anyway, modern fandom has really
been around since the 1890s

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:41.130
when Sherlock Holmes captivated

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.130
the emerging urban middle class.

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:46.610
Holmes fans even protested
when Sir Arthur Conan Doyle

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.540
killed off the beloved detective,
so much so that the author

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.810
begrudgingly brought him back to life.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.930
While the term "fandom"
was applied to sports

00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:55.960
and theater lovers
throughout the 19th century,

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.280
sci-fi literature lovers appropriated it

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:01.000
as they developed "much of
the fannish infrastructure,

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.350
jargon and language
still used to this day,

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.420
according to scholar Francesca Coppa.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:07.270
Sci-fi fans started communicating

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:10.960
via the letters column of famed
magazine, Amazing Stories.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.270
Soon, they began to
produce their own zines.

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:16.540
Was this geeky behavior? For sure.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.720
But according to scholar Lars Konzack,

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.280
geek dom as we know it
today really can be credited

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.610
to the culture built by
World War II veterans,

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.120
especially engineers and
intelligence officers.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:27.690
When veterans returned from war

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:30.000
and began their GI
Bill-funded higher education,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:31.770
they had the time and space to process

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:32.800
what they'd been through.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.700
As Konzack explains, they
expressed their reaction

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.520
to their unique experience
by creating and embracing

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.960
science fiction novels,
superhero comic books,

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.933
and military strategy board games.

00:02:42.933 --> 00:02:46.130
"This," Knozack says, "coalesced
into its own, distinctive

00:02:46.130 --> 00:02:49.880
alternative intellectual milieu
around the Cold War era."

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:52.700
But modern geeky fandom was
poised to really explode

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:57.010
around 1966, when sci-fi made
the jump from books and movies

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:59.200
to the more accessible TV format.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:00.256
And fans of Star Trek,
arguably set the blueprint

00:03:00.256 --> 00:03:03.740
for geek fandom as we know it today.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:05.210
Belittled by the mainstream,

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:07.140
they formed a subculture of their own

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:08.340
to express their passion.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.430
Born in part out of the
counterculture movements

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:12.694
to the 1960s, Trekkies
were super grassroots

00:03:12.694 --> 00:03:14.000
and self-organized.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.800
They created dedicated conventions,

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:17.750
popularized fan-fic and cosplay,

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:20.530
and, some argue, got
their show a third season.

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:22.040
As technology progressed,

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.270
creators could bring ever more
engrossing worlds to life.

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:29.240
And then, of course, Star Wars
hit in '77 and spearheaded

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:32.010
the now-ubiquitous
approach to merchandise.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:34.910
By now, fandom had attracted
scholarly attention.

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:38.160
Academics initial reactions
to fandom stereotyped

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:40.530
these new fans, much like
they had sports fans,

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:42.180
as being pathological.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:43.660
It was even commonly suggested

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:45.620
that fans needed psychiatric help.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:47.150
But, that didn't stop them.

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:49.100
By the 1990's, dedicated fandoms

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:51.700
had become pretty commonplace
for geeky fiction,

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:54.180
all following the grassroots
subcultural example

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:55.260
set by Trekkies.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:57.850
The growth of the internet
facilitated greater connection

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:00.320
between fans than the
readers of Amazing Stories

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.040
could have ever imagined.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.960
By this point, it was possible for people

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:06.820
to have been in a fandom
for most of their lives.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.670
And it was around this time
that fan studies scholars

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:12.400
started pushing back against
prevailing stereotypes.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:14.520
Most notably, there was Henry Jenkins,

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.610
an academic and fellow fan.

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:19.480
Frustrated with the mainstream
stereotype of fandoms

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:21.640
as mindless and anti-social consumers

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:23.330
obsessed with immature stories,

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:26.130
Jenkins explored fandoms ethnographically.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:27.860
He framed them as communities

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.570
rooted in creative engagement
with a given media text,

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:31.560
creating new meanings

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.920
to base the social actions
of the subculture on.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:37.000
Key to this is a
conflicting set of emotions.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.870
To Jenkins, fandom is both fascinated with

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:42.580
and frustrated by the media they focus on.

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:45.340
They're emotionally invested
in the world of the text,

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:47.580
but that investment
also breeds frustration

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.760
with the text's inevitable shortcomings.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:53.050
This fuels acts of creation
like fan fiction and art,

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:55.230
also known as Fan Labor.

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:57.090
Through acts of Fan Labor,

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:59.400
fandoms are essentially tailoring the text

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:02.420
to reflect their own tastes,
views and experiences.

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:05.270
Although it's not just
creative interpretation;

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:07.730
it even extends to acts of evangelism,

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:09.590
like when fandoms organize en masse

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:11.610
to save their favorite shows.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:14.670
Jenkins labels this
"participatory culture",

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:16.980
where the public has more
agency in the formation

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:18.460
and definition of their culture.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:20.500
Participatory culture acts as a sort of

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:23.710
grassroots counterpoint to
mindless media consumption.

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:26.210
But, even as smart folks started defending

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:27.370
fans of geek culture,

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:28.830
one thing remained the same,

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:30.810
the mainstream's scorn.

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:33.020
According to scholar Dan Hassler-Forest,

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:35.900
fans had mainly been perceived
by Hollywood film studios

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:39.360
as irritants, niche groups of
excessively invested consumers

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:42.400
whose limited numbers rendered
their voices insignificant.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.470
Films like Tim Burton's 1989 film Batman,

00:05:45.470 --> 00:05:46.950
were thus purposely tailored

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:49.250
to attract the largest
mainstream audience,

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:51.480
rather than to pay homage to fans.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:53.030
According to Hassler-Forest,

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:55.837
that was about to change
with one big-ass franchise,

00:05:55.837 --> 00:05:57.200
"Lord of the Rings".

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:58.560
Director Peter Jackson,

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:01.270
savvily embraced the
simmering fan anticipation

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:02.103
for the film.

00:06:02.103 --> 00:06:04.950
He gave the blogger behind
fan website, "The One Ring",

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:07.320
a private tour of the set,
let her take photographs,

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:08.550
and eventually relied on her

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:11.020
as a main source of
publicity for the sequels.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:13.240
This marked what Hassler-Forest calls,

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:15.860
a shifting relationship
between fan culture

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:17.000
and media industries,

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.490
where fans were addressed not
only as the ideal consumers

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:22.620
of a commercial fantastic franchise

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.720
but also as valuable
contributors to the process.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:27.610
With the commercial success
of Lord of the Rings,

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:29.910
fans started being viewed as influencers,

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:30.980
who could produce hype

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:33.490
that would ideally bring
in an even larger audience.

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:36.250
This potential mushroomed
as the internet expanded

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:37.540
and media industries started

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:40.245
to rely more and more
on networked cultures

00:06:40.245 --> 00:06:43.210
and spreadable media,
according to Hassler-Forest.

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:45.730
What's more, performing fannish behavior

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:48.790
has become less taboo,
which Hassler-Forest argues

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:51.210
is in part due to the
radical individualization

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.170
that marks our social media environment.

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:56.280
As a result, a lot more
people are doing fandom.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:59.070
And there's every reason to
think fans will only continue

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:00.218
to become more important
as Hollywood keeps chasing

00:07:00.218 --> 00:07:03.290
the most profitable IP.

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:06.360
Some scholars think fandom
will become so mainstream

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:07.700
as to render the distinction

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:10.440
between fan and average audience member

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:12.100
virtually insignificant.

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:14.840
Now, mainstream media didn't
start embracing fandom

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:16.360
out of the goodness of their hearts.

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.500
Stats show that the most
dedicated fans of TV and movies

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:22.210
spend around 45% more than the rest,

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:25.500
for gamers, it's 230%.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:28.860
What's more, fans vastly
outnumber casual viewers.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:30.780
Couple that with fans' zealous dedication

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:32.430
to hyping their favorite franchises,

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:35.030
and you can see why no red-blooded exec

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:36.900
could possibly keep ignoring them.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:38.780
Over the years, geek fandoms have proven

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:41.690
to be a valuable commodity
to media corporations,

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:45.160
Which has meant a fundamental
shift in how fandoms form.

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:47.940
Corporations are now actively
looking to create fandoms

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:50.090
from day one, with different tactics

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:51.690
for different stages of fandom,

00:07:51.690 --> 00:07:54.230
all to stir fans into the most passionate,

00:07:54.230 --> 00:07:55.910
and profitable stay.

00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:57.960
At the same time, geek culture is becoming

00:07:57.960 --> 00:07:59.740
increasingly corporatized.

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:02.620
Conventions like New York
Comic Con and Chicago Comic

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:04.370
and Entertainment expo, and so on

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:06.240
are being bought up by ReedPop,

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:09.630
a subsidiary of global events
organizer Reed Exhibitions,

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:12.678
which continues to engulf ever
more once-grassroots events.

00:08:12.678 --> 00:08:16.230
At the same time, film studios
and media conglomerates

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:18.610
have increasingly
infiltrated these events,

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:21.310
luring fans with exciting
exclusive previews

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:23.120
that they'll be sure to tweet about.

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:25.610
And now, media corporations
have found ways

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:29.560
to commodify fandom itself
by exploiting Fan Labor.

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:31.140
This takes myriad forms,

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:33.390
Like competitions, where the prize

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:36.330
is your fan-art being used
as promotional material,

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:37.700
or being featured in the movie,

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:40.300
that's also known as unpaid labor,

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:42.528
Or leveraging IP law
against free fan-works,

00:08:42.528 --> 00:08:45.110
like short films or game mods,

00:08:45.110 --> 00:08:47.660
only to release their
own versions soon after.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:49.659
Even fan-fic, is being exploited.

00:08:49.659 --> 00:08:53.030
Take shipping, the practice
of creating fan-fic

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:55.507
around theoretical
romantic pairings in an IP.

00:08:55.507 --> 00:08:57.860
Historically a way for queer fans

00:08:57.860 --> 00:08:59.820
to create their own representation,

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:02.900
popular ships are now being
incorporated into marketing

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:06.340
and even alluded to in
the texts themselves,

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:08.139
just to get fans in the door.

00:09:08.139 --> 00:09:10.510
But often, it's a trick,

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:12.370
these ships are largely ignored

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:14.430
in favor of straight romances.

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:16.880
This isn't necessarily
the end of the world,

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:18.790
but the iron grip corporations

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:22.260
are starting to have on fandoms
arguably creates problems.

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:24.890
When a corporation all
but controls a subculture,

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:27.158
can you really call it
a subculture anymore?

00:09:27.158 --> 00:09:29.350
And what happens when a subculture,

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:30.980
used to operating as outcasts

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:32.450
in opposition to the mainstream,

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:35.830
finds their forum confined
by corporate oversight?

00:09:35.830 --> 00:09:38.730
Similarly, subcultures like
those surrounding indie rock,

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:42.020
punk, and emo music have
encountered these same tensions.

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:44.860
Essentially, fandoms used
to play in the woods,

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:46.730
but corporations have paved them over

00:09:46.730 --> 00:09:48.780
with a high-fenced jungle gym.

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:50.788
The question, then, poses itself,

00:09:50.788 --> 00:09:52.519
when fandom is mainstream,

00:09:52.519 --> 00:09:55.480
does it even really exist anymore?

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:57.530
Fans may be courted by corporations,

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:59.480
but do they actually have power,

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:01.840
especially when a few media conglomerates

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:04.980
own almost all of popular culture's IP?

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:06.773
It depends who you ask.

00:10:06.773 --> 00:10:08.880
From Jenkins' interpretation,

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:10.709
fandom could be seen as an active way

00:10:10.709 --> 00:10:13.520
to invert the increased
consolidation of IP.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:16.000
He argued that fan-fic in particular,

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:18.950
is a way of the culture
repairing the damage done

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:20.960
in a system where contemporary myths

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:24.230
are owned by corporations
instead of owned by the folk.

00:10:24.230 --> 00:10:27.038
This interpretation sees
fans as active creators

00:10:27.038 --> 00:10:29.460
and consumers of their
favorite geek media,

00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:31.370
rather than passive receptacles

00:10:31.370 --> 00:10:34.280
for whatever corporate
media wants them to stan.

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:37.140
This is interesting, because
Jenkins acknowledges,

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:39.900
that the media that fandoms
form around are essentially,

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:41.210
corporate products.

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:43.210
Regardless of the empowering aspects

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:45.550
of Jenkins' proposed
participatory culture,

00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:47.640
fandoms are still emotionally invested

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:50.320
in something that
corporations make for profit.

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:51.670
Which is why others argue

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:54.410
that the fan autonomy
celebrated by Jenkins

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:56.120
is something of an illusion.

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:59.210
Some of this has to do with
the power of the mighty IP.

00:10:59.210 --> 00:11:00.227
As Simone Murray puts it,

00:11:00.227 --> 00:11:02.640
"The ultimate discretion
of media conglomerates

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:05.750
to shut down fan creations
around corporate-owned properties

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:08.100
renders hollow much cultural studies

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:10.930
champion of fans' contrarian impulse".

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:13.660
That is to say, when
Disney ,by way of Marvel,

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:15.700
can sue you for using too many clips

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:17.410
in your reaction video to Ant-Man,

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:20.130
can you actually take
culture into your own hands?

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:22.650
According to Hassler-Forest, not really.

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:26.090
He argues that there is a
grossly asymmetrical relationship

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:28.180
between fans and media producers,

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:31.420
such that fan autonomy
becomes somewhat illusory.

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:32.253
He writes that,

00:11:32.253 --> 00:11:33.250
when beloved stories

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:36.060
become highly profitable
global entertainment brands,

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:38.610
they no longer make up a
form of cultural commons

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:41.330
that can be appropriated,
embellished, transformed,

00:11:41.330 --> 00:11:42.163
or subverted.

00:11:42.163 --> 00:11:44.660
Instead, they become
lucrative corporate franchises

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:46.540
made up of a wide range of commodities,

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:48.800
most of which are dependent upon fans

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:50.656
to achieve their desired commercial value.

00:11:50.656 --> 00:11:52.570
That is to say, media producers

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:55.600
have both the power to stop
fans from circulating their arts

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:58.580
and the resources to produce
a gob smacking amount

00:11:58.580 --> 00:11:59.850
of content and products.

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:01.610
In both limiting fan production

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:03.883
and aggressively
encouraging fan consumption,

00:12:03.883 --> 00:12:07.160
media producers are arguably
co-opting fan culture

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:09.070
while stymieing its expression.

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:10.700
What this argument really spotlights,

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:13.130
is the tension between
the two sides of fandom,

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:15.380
fans as anti-commercial subcultures

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:16.910
organized on a grassroots level,

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:20.380
and fans as rabid consumers
and promoters of corporate IP.

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:23.170
And as fandom becomes
increasingly mainstream,

00:12:23.170 --> 00:12:25.060
that tension might just constitute

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:26.890
one of the most important debates

00:12:26.890 --> 00:12:28.718
about how we consume media.

00:12:28.718 --> 00:12:30.410
But what do you guys think?

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:33.250
Has corporate involvement changed
what it means to be a fan?

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:35.300
Is there still room for geek culture?

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:36.730
Let us know in the comments.

